rl. | Link Pred | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | Political |\n| US Legis. | Link Pred | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | Political |\n| UN Trade | Link Pred | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | Economics |\n| UN Vote | Link Pred | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | Political |\n| Contact | Link Pred | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | 0.XXX | Social |\n\n**Note**: Exact values available in paper: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.08309\n\n**Key Findings from Paper**:\n1. L-STEP achieves state-of-the-art performance on link prediction benchmarks\n2. FFT-based positional encoding improves temporal pattern capture\n3. Learnable PE outperforms fixed positional encodings\n4. Effective on both small and large-scale graphs\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Comparison with Baselines (From Paper)\n\n**Compared Methods** (Link Prediction Models):\n- TGAT (Temporal Graph Attention)\n- DyGFormer (Dynamic Graph Transformer)\n- CAWN (Context-Aware Weighted Network)\n- GraphMixer\n- TCL (Temporal Consistency Learning)\n- EdgeBank\n\n**L-STEP Improvements**:\n- Average MRR improvement: ~X% over best baseline\n- Particularly strong on small-scale datasets\n- Learnable PE provides consistent gains\n\n**Note**: These are link prediction baselines, NOT traffic speed prediction models.\n\n---\n\n## Section 3: Metric Comparison Analysis\n\n### 3.1 LibCity vs. Paper Metrics\n\n**COMPARISON NOT POSSIBLE** - Different tasks, different metrics\n\n#### Why Comparison is Impossible\n\n| Aspect | LibCity (Expected) | L-STEP (Actual) | Compatible? |\n|--------|-------------------|-----------------|-------------|\n| **Task** | Speed Prediction | Link Prediction | ❌ NO |\n| **Metrics** | MAE, RMSE, MAPE | MRR, Hits@k | ❌ NO |\n| **Output** | Continuous values | Binary/Ranking | ❌ NO |\n| **Datasets** | PEMS, METR-LA | Wikipedia, Reddit | ❌ NO |\n| **Evaluation** |Regression error | Ranking quality | ❌ NO |\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 Metric Definitions\n\n#### Expected Metrics (Traffic Speed - Not Available)\n\n**MAE (Mean Absolute Error)**:\n```\nMAE = (1/N) Σ |y_pred - y_true|\nLower is better\nUnit: Same as speed (km/h or mph)\n```\n\n**MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error)**:\n```\nMAPE = (100/N) Σ |y_pred - y_true| / |y_true|\nLower is better\nUnit: Percentage (%)\n```\n\n**RMSE (Root Mean Square Error)**:\n```\nRMSE = sqrt((1/N) Σ (y_pred - y_true)²)\nLower is better\nUnit: Same as speed (km/h or mph)\n```\n\n**MASE (Mean Absolute Scaled Error)**:\n```\nMASE = MAE / MAE_naive\nLower is better (< 1 means better than naive baseline)\n```\n\n#### Actual Metrics (Link Prediction - Available in Paper)\n\n**MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank)**:\n```\nMRR = (1/N) Σ (1 / rank_i)\nHigher is better\nRange: [0, 1]\n```\n\n**Hits@k**:\n```\nHits@k = (Number of correct predictions in top-k) / N\nHigher is better\nRange: [0, 1]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3.3 Performance Delta Analysis\n\n**Status**: ❌ **NOT APPLICABLE**\n\nSince L-STEP was never tested on traffic datasets and produces different metrics, performance delta analysis cannot be performed.\n\n**What we would analyze (if model were compatible)**:\n- LibCity MAE vs. Paper MAE\n- LibCity MAPE vs. Paper MAPE\n- LibCity RMSE vs. Paper RMSE\n- Reasons for differences (data preprocessing, hyperparameters, etc.)\n\n**What we cannot analyze**:\n- Link prediction metrics (MRR, Hits@k) cannot be compared with regression metrics (MAE, RMSE, MAPE)\n- Different problem formulations make comparison meaningless\n\n---\n\n## Section 4: Incompatibility Root Cause Analysis\n\n### 4.1 Task Formulation Difference\n\n#### L-STEP (Link Prediction)\n```python\n# Problem formulation\nInput: Graph G_t = (V, E_t) at time t\nTask: Predict probability of edge (u, v) at time t+1\nOutput: P(edge exists) ∈ [0, 1]\nLoss: Binary cross-entropy or ranking loss\nEvaluation: MRR, Hits@k\n\nExample:\n  Input: User interaction graph at time t\n  Output: Probability that users A and B will interact at t+1\n```\n\n#### LibCity Traffic (Speed Prediction)\n```python\n# Problem formulation\nInput: Speed observations X_t ∈ ℝ^{N×F} at time t\nTask: Predict future speed values Y_{t+1:t+h}\nOutput: Speed values ∈ ℝ^{N×h}\nLoss: MSE, MAE\nEvaluation: MAE, RMSE, MAPE\n\nExample:\n  Input: Speed readings on road segments at time t\n  Output: Predicted speed values at time t+1, t+2, ..., t+h\n```\n\n**Fundamental Difference**: Binary/Ranking vs. Continuous Regression\n\n---\n\n### 4.2 Data Structure Difference\n\n#### L-STEP Data Format\n```python\nclass Data:\n    src_node_ids: np.ndarray        # [E] source nodes\n    dst_node_ids: np.ndarray        # [E] destination nodes\n    node_interact_times: np.ndarray # [E] interaction timestamps\n    edge_ids: np.ndarray            # [E] edge IDs\n    labels: np.ndarray              # [E] binary labels\n\n# Example: Email network\nsrc = [user_1, user_3, user_2, ...]  # Who sent email\ndst = [user_2, user_5, user_7, ...]  # Who received\ntimes = [10:30, 11:45, 14:20, ...]   # When\nlabels = [1, 1, 1, ...]              # Edge exists\n```\n\n#### LibCity Data Format\n```python\nclass Batch:\n    X: torch.Tensor  # [B, T_in, N, F]  - Input features\n    y: torch.Tensor  # [B, T_out, N, 1] - Target speeds\n\n# Example: Traffic speed\nX = [[[sensor_1_speed, sensor_2_speed, ...],  # Time t-11\n      [sensor_1_speed, sensor_2_speed, ...],  # Time t-10\n      ...\n      [sensor_1_speed, sensor_2_speed, ...]]] # Time t\n\ny = [[[sensor_1_speed],  # Time t+1\n      [sensor_2_speed],\n      ...]]\n```\n\n**Fundamental Difference**: Event-based sparse graph vs. Regular dense grid\n\n---\n\n### 4.3 Why Metrics Differ\n\n| Reason | Explanation | Impact |\n|--------|-------------|--------|\n| **Different Tasks** | Link prediction vs. regression | Complete metric incompatibility |\n| **Different Outputs** | Probabilities vs. continuous values | Cannot use same evaluation |\n| **Different Objectives** | Ranking quality vs. prediction error | Different optimization goals |\n| **Different Datasets** | Graphs vs. time series | No shared evaluation protocol |\n\n---\n\n## Section 5: Corrective Action Analysis\n\n### 5.1 Potential Fixes Evaluated\n\n#### Fix 1: Adapt Model Architecture\n**Attempted**: No\n**Feasibility**: ❌ Not feasible\n**Reason**: Would require complete redesign\n**Effort**: Months of development\n**Outcome**: Would create a new model, not a port\n\n#### Fix 2: Convert Data Format\n**Attempted**: No\n**Feasibility**: ❌ Not feasible\n**Reason**: Semantic incompatibility\n**Effort**: Impossible - different data semantics\n**Outcome**: Cannot convert traffic grids to graph edges meaningfully\n\n#### Fix 3: Use Different Datasets\n**Attempted**: No\n**Feasibility**: ❌ Not feasible\n**Reason**: No traffic datasets in L-STEP\n**Effort**: Would require retraining from scratch\n**Outcome**: No pretrained weights for traffic\n\n#### Fix 4: Change Evaluation Metrics\n**Attempted**: No\n**Feasibility**: ❌ Not feasible\n**Reason**: Metrics tied to task type\n**Effort**: Meaningless - ranking metrics don\'t apply to regression\n**Outcome**: Would provide incorrect evaluation\n\n---\n\n### 5.2 Corrective Steps Conclusion\n\n**Summary**: ❌ **NO CORRECTIVE STEPS POSSIBLE**\n\nAll potential fixes require fundamental changes that would result in creating a new model rather than porting L-STEP. The incompatibility is at the problem formulation level, not implementation details.\n\n---\n\n## Section 6: Comparison Table Summary\n\n### 6.1 Model Performance Comparison\n\nSince L-STEP cannot be compared with traffic speed models (different tasks), we provide a reference table showing what a comparison would look like:\n\n#### Traffic Speed Models (Reference)\n\n| Model | Source | METR-LA MAE | PEMS-BAY MAE | Task |\n|-------|--------|-------------|--------------|------|\n| RSTIB | ICML 2025 | 2.XX | 1.XX | Speed Pred ✅ |\n| GWNET | ICML 2019 | 2.69 | 1.30 | Speed Pred ✅ |\n| DCRNN | ICLR 2018 | 2.77 | 1.38 | Speed Pred ✅ |\n| **L-STEP** | **ICML 2025** | **N/A** | **N/A** | **Link Pred ❌** |\n\n**L-STEP Performance**: N/A (different task - uses MRR, Hits@k on graph datasets)\n\n---\n\n### 6.2 Metric Comparison Matrix\n\n| Model | Task | Primary Metric | METR-LA | PEMS-BAY | Datasets |\n|-------|------|----------------|---------|----------|----------|\n| RSTIB | Speed Pred | MAE ↓ | ✅ 2.XX | ✅ 1.XX | PEMS ✅ |\n| Timer-XL | Speed Pred | MAE ↓ | ✅ Available | ✅ Available | PEMS ✅ |\n| L-STEP | Link Pred | MRR ↑ | ❌ N/A | ❌ N/A | Wikipedia, Reddit ❌ |\n\n**Legend**:\n- ✅ = Metric available and comparable\n- ❌ = Metric not available / not applicable\n- ↓ = Lower is better\n- ↑ = Higher is better\n\n---\n\n### 6.3 Dataset Coverage Comparison\n\n| Dataset | Type | RSTIB | GriddedTNP | L-STEP | Task Compatible? |\n|---------|------|-------|------------|--------|------------------|\n| METR-LA | Traffic | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | N/A |\n| PEMS-BAY | Traffic | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | N/A |\n| PEMS04 | Traffic | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | N/A |\n| Wikipedia | Graph | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | Different task |\n| Reddit | Graph | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | Different task |\n\n**Overlap**: 0% between L-STEP and traffic datasets\n\n---\n\n## Section 7: Divergence Analysis\n\n### 7.1 Why Results Diverge (N/A for L-STEP)\n\n**This section would analyze**:\n- Data preprocessing differences\n- Hyperparameter variations\n- Missing features\n- Implementation bugs\n- Dataset splits\n\n**Status**: ❌ Not applicable - model never tested on traffic data\n\n---\n\n### 7.2 Potential Divergence Factors (If Model Were Compatible)\n\nIf L-STEP were a traffic model, potential divergence factors would include:\n\n| Factor | Impact | Mitigation |\n|--------|--------|------------|\n| Data preprocessing | High | Match paper\'s preprocessing |\n| Hyperparameters | Medium | Use paper\'s best config |\n| Random seed | Low | Average over multiple runs |\n| Framework version | Medium | Match PyTorch version |\n| Hardware | Low | Minimal impact |\n\n**Actual Status**: Not applicable - task incompatibility prevents testing\n\n---\n\n## Section 8: Recommended Follow-up Experiments\n\n### 8.1 Experiments for L-STEP (Not Recommended)\n\n**Status**: ❌ **NOT RECOMMENDED**\n\nNo follow-up experiments recommended for L-STEP integration with LibCity traffic speed prediction due to fundamental incompatibility.\n\n---\n\n### 8.2 Alternative Experiments (Recommended)\n\n#### Option 1: Extract Learnable Components ⚠️\n**Goal**: Use L-STEP\'s positional encoding concepts in traffic models\n**Effort**: Medium-High\n**Benefit**: Potential improvement to existing traffic models\n**Steps**:\n1. Extract TimeEncoder module\n2. Adapt FFT-based aggregation for spatiotemporal grids\n3. Test on existing LibCity models (GWNET, DCRNN)\n4. Measure performance impact\n\n**Expected Outcome**: Minor to moderate performance gains\n\n---\n\n#### Option 2: Graph-Based Traffic Analysis 🔬\n**Goal**: Use L-STEP for different traffic tasks\n**Effort**: High\n**Benefit**: New insights into traffic network dynamics\n**Steps**:\n1. Formulate traffic as graph link prediction problem\n   - Example: Predict when congestion will spread to adjacent roads\n2. Convert traffic data to graph edges\n   - Example: Edge exists if roads share congestion pattern\n3. Train L-STEP on this new formulation\n4. Evaluate with link prediction metrics\n\n**Expected Outcome**: Novel traffic pattern analysis (not speed prediction)\n\n---\n\n#### Option 3: Focus on Compatible Models ✅ (RECOMMENDED)\n**Goal**: Migrate actual traffic speed models\n**Effort**: Low-Medium per model\n**Benefit**: Direct traffic speed prediction improvements\n**Priority Models**:\n\n1. **Timer-XL** (ICLR 2025)\n   - Status: Ready for migration\n   - Datasets: PEMS (explicit support)\n   - Expected MAE: Competitive with SOTA\n\n2. **SimpleTM** (ICLR 2025)\n   - Status: Ready for migration\n   - Datasets: Traffic (862 variables)\n   - Expected MAE: Strong performance\n\n3. **STELLA** (NeurIPS 2025)\n   - Status: Ready for migration\n   - Datasets: Atmospheric (similar to traffic)\n   - Expected MAE: Good generalization\n\n**Expected Outcome**: Measurable traffic speed prediction improvements\n\n---\n\n### 8.3 Experiment Priority Ranking\n\n| Experiment | Priority | Effort | Benefit | Status |\n|------------|----------|--------|---------|--------|\n| Migrate Timer-XL | 🔴 High | Medium | High | ✅ Recommended |\n| Migrate SimpleTM | 🔴 High | Medium | High | ✅ Recommended |\n| Migrate STELLA | 🟡 Medium | Medium | Medium | ✅ Recommended |\n| Extract L-STEP components | 🟡 Medium | High | Low-Medium | ⚠️ Optional |\n| Graph-based traffic | 🟢 Low | High | Low | 🔬 Research |\n| Force L-STEP integration | ⚫ None | Impossible | None | ❌ Not recommended |\n\n---\n\n## Section 9: Lessons Learned & Best Practices\n\n### 9.1 Catalog Filtering Lessons\n\n**Issue**: L-STEP included in traffic model catalog despite being link prediction model\n\n**Root Cause**:\n- Keywords "spatial-temporal" misleading\n- No explicit task type filtering\n- Conference/venue not indicative of task\n\n**Recommendation**:\n1. Add explicit "task_type" field to catalog\n2. Filter by actual datasets used (PEMS = traffic)\n3. Check evaluation metrics (MAE/RMSE = traffic, MRR = link prediction)\n4. Verify problem formulation before inclusion\n\n---\n\n### 9.2 Pre-Integration Checklist\n\nBefore attempting future model migrations, verify:\n\n- [ ] **Task Type Match**\n  - Model task = LibCity task ✅\n  - Example: Regression = Regression ✅\n\n- [ ] **Dataset Compatibility**\n  - Model datasets ∈ {PEMS, METR-LA, traffic datasets} ✅\n  - Example: PEMS04 ∈ model datasets ✅\n\n- [ ] **Metric Compatibility**\n  - Model metrics ∈ {MAE, RMSE, MAPE} ✅\n  - Example: MAE reported in paper ✅\n\n- [ ] **Data Format**\n  - Model input = Spatiotemporal grid ✅\n  - Example: (batch, time, nodes, features) ✅\n\n- [ ] **Output Format**\n  - Model output = Continuous values ✅\n  - Example: Speed predictions ∈ ℝ ✅\n\n**L-STEP Checklist Results**:\n- [ ] ❌ Task type: Link prediction ≠ Speed prediction\n- [ ] ❌ Datasets: Wikipedia, Reddit ≠ PEMS\n- [ ] ❌ Metrics: MRR, Hits@k ≠ MAE, RMSE\n- [ ] ❌ Data format: Graph edges ≠ Spatiotemporal grid\n- [ ] ❌ Output: Probabilities ≠ Speed values\n\n**Result**: 0/5 checks passed → Migration not feasible\n\n---\n\n## Section 10: Final Summary\n\n### 10.1 Metrics Summary Table\n\n| Metric Category | Status | Values | Comparison |\n|----------------|--------|--------|------------|\n| **Traffic Speed (Expected)** | ❌ N/A | - | - |\n| MAE | Not tested | N/A | N/A |\n| MAPE | Not tested | N/A | N/A |\n| RMSE | Not tested | N/A | N/A |\n| MASE | Not tested | N/A | N/A |\n| **Link Prediction (Actual)** | ✅ Available | See paper | Not comparable |\n| MRR | In paper | ~0.XXX | - |\n| Hits@1 | In paper | ~0.XXX | - |\n| Hits@10 | In paper | ~0.XXX | - |\n\n---\n\n### 10.2 Comparison with Paper\n\n**Comparison Status**: ❌ **NOT POSSIBLE**\n\n**Reason**: Different tasks produce different metrics that cannot be compared.\n\n**Paper Results**: Link prediction performance (MRR, Hits@k) on graph datasets\n\n**Expected LibCity Results**: Traffic speed prediction performance (MAE, RMSE, MAPE) on PEMS datasets\n\n**Overlap**: None - completely different evaluation protocols\n\n---\n\n### 10.3 Divergence Summary\n\n| Divergence Type | Status | Analysis |\n|----------------|--------|----------|\n| Metric values | N/A | No shared metrics |\n| Dataset performance | N/A | No shared datasets |\n| Hyperparameters | N/A | Never tested |\n| Preprocessing | N/A | Different data types |\n\n**Conclusion**: No divergence to analyze - tasks are incompatible\n\n---\n\n### 10.4 Recommendations Summary\n\n#### ❌ Do NOT:\n1. Attempt to force L-STEP integration with LibCity traffic speed\n2. Try to compare link prediction metrics with regression metrics\n3. Waste time on incompatible migration\n\n#### ✅ DO:\n1. **Mark L-STEP as "Not Applicable"** for traffic speed prediction\n2. **Focus on compatible models**: Timer-XL, SimpleTM, STELLA\n3. **Use RSTIB** as example of successful migration\n4. **Optionally extract** learnable components for other uses\n\n#### 🔬 Research Opportunities:\n1. Apply L-STEP to graph-based traffic problems (not speed prediction)\n2. Study FFT-based temporal aggregation for inspiration\n3. Extract TimeEncoder for reuse in traffic models\n\n---\n\n## Section 11: Detailed Recommendations Table\n\n### 11.1 Model Migration Priorities\n\n| Model | Conference | Task | Traffic Data | Priority | Expected MAE |\n|-------|-----------|------|--------------|----------|--------------|\n| **Timer-XL** | ICLR 2025 | TS Forecast | ✅ PEMS | 🔴 High | Competitive |\n| **SimpleTM** | ICLR 2025 | TS Forecast | ✅ Traffic | 🔴 High | Good |\n| **STELLA** | NeurIPS 2025 | ST Forecast | ⚠️ Atmospheric | 🟡 Medium | Moderate |\n| **L-STEP** | ICML 2025 | Link Pred | ❌ Social | ⚫ None | N/A |\n\n---\n\n### 11.2 Follow-up Experiments\n\n| Experiment | Type | Effort | Timeline | Success Probability |\n|------------|------|--------|----------|---------------------|\n| Migrate Timer-XL | Integration | Medium | 2-3 days | 90% |\n| Migrate SimpleTM | Integration | Medium | 2-3 days | 85% |\n| Migrate STELLA | Integration | Medium | 3-4 days | 75% |\n| Extract TimeEncoder | Component | Low | 1 day | 95% |\n| Graph traffic analysis | Research | High | 2+ weeks | 50% |\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\n**L-STEP Metrics Status**: ❌ **NOT AVAILABLE** - Fundamental task incompatibility\n\n**Key Findings**:\n1. L-STEP is a link prediction model, not traffic speed prediction\n2. No traffic speed metrics (MAE, MAPE, RMSE) can be generated\n3. Model uses different metrics (MRR, Hits@k) for different task\n4. No comparison with traffic baselines possible\n5. Migration to LibCity traffic_speed_prediction not feasible\n\n**Recommendations**:\n1. ✅ Preserve L-STEP repository for reference\n2. ✅ Focus on compatible models (Timer-XL, SimpleTM, STELLA)\n3. ✅ Use comprehensive documentation for future reference\n4. ❌ Do not attempt traffic speed integration\n\n**Next Steps**:\n- Proceed with Timer-XL or SimpleTM migration\n- Use RSTIB as successful integration template\n- Apply pre-integration checklist for future models\n\n---\n\n**Report Generated**: 2025-11-25\n**Status**: ✅ Complete\n**Conclusion**: No traffic speed metrics available due to fundamental task incompatibility\n\n---\n\n## Appendix: Metric Formulas Reference\n\n### Traffic Speed Metrics (Expected but Not Available)\n\n**Masked MAE**:\n```python\ndef masked_mae(y_true, y_pred, null_val=0.0):\n    mask = (y_true != null_val)\n    mask = mask.float()\n    mask /= mask.mean()\n    mae = torch.abs(y_pred - y_true)\n    mae = mae * mask\n    mae = mae.mean()\n    return mae\n```\n\n**Masked MAPE**:\n```python\ndef masked_mape(y_true, y_pred, null_val=0.0):\n    mask = (y_true != null_val)\n    mask = mask.float()\n    mask /= mask.mean()\n    mape = torch.abs((y_pred - y_true) / y_true)\n    mape = mape * mask\n    mape = mape.mean()\n    return mape * 100\n```\n\n**Masked RMSE**:\n```python\ndef masked_rmse(y_true, y_pred, null_val=0.0):\n    mask = (y_true != null_val)\n    mask = mask.float()\n    mask /= mask.mean()\n    mse = (y_pred - y_true) ** 2\n    mse = mse * mask\n    rmse = torch.sqrt(mse.mean())\n    return rmse\n```\n\n**Status**: Not computed - model incompatible with traffic data\n\n---\n\n### Link Prediction Metrics (Available in Paper)\n\n**MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank)**:\n```python\ndef compute_mrr(ranks):\n    """\n    ranks: List of ranks for correct predictions\n    """\n    reciprocal_ranks = 1.0 / ranks\n    mrr = np.mean(reciprocal_ranks)\n    return mrr\n```\n\n**Hits@k**:\n```python\ndef compute_hits_at_k(ranks, k):\n    """\n    ranks: List of ranks for correct predictions\n    k: Cutoff threshold\n    """\n    hits = (ranks <= k).astype(float)\n    return np.mean(hits)\n```\n\n**Status**: Used by L-STEP for link prediction evaluation\n\n---\n\n**End of Metrics & Analysis Report**\n'}
