2025-11-26 07:11:36 | INFO | Tool Write called with {'file_path': '/home/wangwenrui/private/shk/codex/documentation/EAC_Integration_Guide.md', 'content': '# EAC Model Integration Guide\n\n## Overview\n\nThis document describes the integration of the **EAC (Expand and Compress)** model into LibCity framework.\n\n- **Paper**: Expand and Compress: Exploring Tuning Principles for Continual Spatio-Temporal Graph Forecasting\n- **Venue**: ICLR 2025\n- **Original Repository**: https://github.com/Onedean/EAC\n- **Paper Link**: https://openreview.net/pdf?id=FRzCIlkM7I\n\n## Model Description\n\nEAC is a continual learning method for spatio-temporal graph forecasting that addresses:\n- Inefficiency of retraining models over newly-arrived data\n- Catastrophic forgetting over long-term history\n\nThe model uses prompt tuning with **expand** and **compress** principles:\n- **Expand**: Low-rank adaptive parameters (U, V) to capture new patterns\n- **Compress**: Temporal and spatial graph convolutions with efficient parameter sharing\n\n## Files Modified/Created\n\n### 1. Model Implementation\n- **File**: `Bigscity-LibCity/libcity/model/traffic_speed_prediction/EAC.py`\n- **Description**: Main model implementation adapted for LibCity framework\n- **Key Components**:\n  - `BatchGCNConv`: Graph convolution layer for batch processing\n  - `EACCore`: Core model with expand/compress mechanism\n  - `EAC`: LibCity wrapper implementing AbstractTrafficStateModel\n\n### 2. Model Registry\n- **File**: `Bigscity-LibCity/libcity/model/traffic_speed_prediction/__init__.py`\n- **Changes**:\n  - Added `from libcity.model.traffic_speed_prediction.EAC import EAC`\n  - Added `"EAC"` to `__all__` list\n\n### 3. Configuration File\n- **File**: `Bigscity-LibCity/libcity/config/model/traffic_state_pred/EAC.json`\n- **Description**: Model configuration parameters\n- **Key Parameters**:\n  - `rank`: 6 (low-rank dimension for expand/compress)\n  - `gcn_hidden_channel`: 64\n  - `dropout`: 0.0\n  - `learning_rate`: 0.03\n\n## Model Architecture\n\n```\nInput [B, T_in, N, F]\n    ↓\nReshape to [B, N, T_in * F]\n    ↓\nExpand: Add low-rank adaptive parameters (U @ V)\n    ↓\nGCN Layer 1 (with adjacency matrix)\n    ↓\nTCN Layer (temporal convolution)\n    ↓\nGCN Layer 2\n    ↓\nOutput FC Layer\n    ↓\nOutput [B, T_out, N, 1]\n```\n\nWhere:\n- B: Batch size\n- T_in: Input time steps (e.g., 12)\n- T_out: Output time steps (e.g., 12)\n- N: Number of nodes\n- F: Feature dimension\n\n## Data Format\n\n### Input (batch[\'X\'])\n- **Shape**: `[batch_size, input_window, num_nodes, feature_dim]`\n- **Example**: `[32, 12, 207, 1]` for METR-LA dataset\n\n### Output (batch[\'y\'])\n- **Shape**: `[batch_size, output_window, num_nodes, output_dim]`\n- **Example**: `[32, 12, 207, 1]`\n\n### Adjacency Matrix\n- **Format**: Numpy array of shape `[num_nodes, num_nodes]`\n- **Normalization**: Row-wise normalization (A / rowsum(A))\n- **Source**: From `data_feature[\'adj_mx\']`\n\n## Configuration Parameters\n\n### Model-Specific Parameters\n```json\n{\n  "rank": 6,                    # Low-rank dimension for expand/compress\n  "gcn_hidden_channel": 64,     # Hidden dimension in GCN layers\n  "gcn_out_channel": 12,        # Output dimension of GCN\n  "tcn_kernel_size": 3,         # Temporal convolution kernel size\n  "tcn_dilation": 1,            # Temporal convolution dilation\n  "dropout": 0.0                # Dropout rate\n}\n```\n\n### Training Parameters\n```json\n{\n  "max_epoch": 100,\n  "learning_rate": 0.03,\n  "learner": "adam",\n  "lr_scheduler": "exponentiallr",\n  "lr_decay_ratio": 0.95,\n  "clip_grad_norm": true,\n  "max_grad_norm": 5\n}\n```\n\n## Usage Example\n\n### Training via LibCity CLI\n\n```bash\npython run_model.py --task traffic_state_pred --model EAC --dataset METR_LA\n```\n\n### With Custom Configuration\n\n```bash\npython run_model.py --task traffic_state_pred --model EAC --dataset METR_LA \\\n    --rank 8 --gcn_hidden_channel 128 --learning_rate 0.01\n```\n\n### Programmatic Usage\n\n```python\nfrom libcity.config import ConfigParser\nfrom libcity.data import get_dataset\nfrom libcity.model import get_model\n\n# Load configuration\nconfig = ConfigParser(task=\'traffic_state_pred\', model=\'EAC\', dataset=\'METR_LA\')\n\n# Load dataset\ndataset = get_dataset(config)\ntrain_data, valid_data, test_data = dataset.get_data()\n\n# Create model\nmodel = get_model(config, dataset.get_data_feature())\n\n# Train (via executor)\nfrom libcity.executor import get_executor\nexecutor = get_executor(config, model, dataset)\nexecutor.train(train_data, valid_data)\nexecutor.evaluate(test_data)\n```\n\n## Datasets Tested\n\nThe EAC model was originally tested on:\n1. **PEMS-Stream**: Continual learning traffic dataset (2011-2017)\n2. **METR-LA**: Los Angeles traffic (207 sensors)\n3. **PEMS-BAY**: Bay Area traffic (325 sensors)\n\nAll these datasets are compatible with LibCity\'s data format.\n\n## Key Differences from Original Implementation\n\n### 1. Data Format\n- **Original**: Custom PyTorch Geometric Data format\n- **LibCity**: Standardized batch dictionary with \'X\' and \'y\' keys\n\n### 2. Adjacency Matrix\n- **Original**: Loaded per year for continual learning\n- **LibCity**: Single adjacency matrix from data_feature\n\n### 3. Loss Function\n- **Original**: MSE loss\n- **LibCity**: Masked MAE (masked_mae_torch)\n\n### 4. Continual Learning Features\n- **Original**: Supports dynamic graph expansion with `expand_adaptive_params()`\n- **LibCity**: Method preserved but not automatically triggered (can be extended)\n\n## Extending for Continual Learning\n\nThe model includes the `expand_adaptive_params()` method for continual learning scenarios:\n\n```python\n# To expand model for new nodes\nmodel.model.expand_adaptive_params(new_num_nodes=250)\n```\n\nThis feature can be integrated with LibCity\'s incremental learning framework in future work.\n\n## Performance Considerations\n\n### Memory Usage\n- Low-rank matrices (U, V) keep parameters lightweight\n- Rank=6: ~6 × (N + F) additional parameters\n- Example: METR-LA with rank=6, N=207, F=12: ~1,314 params\n\n### Training Speed\n- Similar to other GCN-based models (DCRNN, STGCN)\n- TCN provides efficient temporal modeling\n- Batch normalization in adjacency reduces overhead\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### Issue: Out of memory\n- **Solution**: Reduce `gcn_hidden_channel` or `rank`\n\n### Issue: Slow convergence\n- **Solution**: Increase `learning_rate` or adjust `lr_scheduler`\n\n### Issue: Poor performance\n- **Solution**:\n  - Check adjacency matrix normalization\n  - Ensure input/output window alignment\n  - Verify data scaling (use standard scaler)\n\n## References\n\n```bibtex\n@inproceedings{chen2025eac,\n  title={Expand and Compress: Exploring Tuning Principles for Continual Spatio-Temporal Graph Forecasting},\n  author={Wei Chen and Yuxuan Liang},\n  booktitle={The Thirteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  year={2025}\n}\n```\n\n## Contact\n\nFor issues related to the LibCity integration, please refer to:\n- LibCity repository: https://github.com/LibCity/Bigscity-LibCity\n- Original EAC repository: https://github.com/Onedean/EAC\n\n## Change Log\n\n### 2025-11-26 - Initial Integration\n- Created EAC.py model implementation\n- Added model registration in __init__.py\n- Created EAC.json configuration file\n- Tested basic instantiation (pending)\n'}
