weight model performance\n- STELLA has 30-40x fewer parameters than GNN models\n- STELLA training is significantly faster (no graph convolutions or attention)\n- Expected accuracy slightly lower than SOTA GNNs but competitive given simplicity\n\n### PEMS-BAY Dataset (12-step prediction horizon)\n\n| Model | MAE ↓ | RMSE ↓ | MAPE (%) ↓ | Parameters | Training Time |\n|-------|-------|--------|------------|------------|---------------|\n| **STELLA (estimated)** | **1.90** | **4.00** | **4.50** | **~14K** | **Fast (~1h)** |\n| GWNET | 1.75 | 3.67 | 3.90 | ~500K | Slow |\n| DCRNN | 1.97 | 4.19 | 4.27 | ~380K | Very Slow |\n| STGCN | 2.49 | 5.69 | 5.79 | ~360K | Medium |\n\n### Original Paper Results (Atmospheric Data)\n\n| Dataset | MAE ↓ | Training Time | Notes |\n|---------|-------|---------------|-------|\n| ChinaWind | **Competitive** | **~2 min** | Outperforms many baselines |\n| ChinaTemp | **Competitive** | **~2 min** | Matches or beats complex models |\n| Global datasets | **Strong** | **Fast** | Good generalization |\n\n**Key Findings from Original Paper**:\n- STELLA achieves competitive accuracy with 10-100x fewer parameters\n- Training is 5-10x faster than Transformer/GNN baselines\n- Works well on large-scale spatial-temporal datasets\n- Designed for atmospheric forecasting (temperature, wind speed)\n\n### Performance Expectations & Adaptations\n\n**Why Traffic Data May Differ from Paper**:\n\n1. **Data Characteristics**:\n   - Original: Smooth atmospheric patterns (temperature, wind)\n   - Traffic: More volatile, sudden changes (accidents, rush hours)\n   - Impact: May need more layers or higher d_model for traffic\n\n2. **Spatial Patterns**:\n   - Original: Geographical distance strongly correlates with similarity\n   - Traffic: Road network topology more important than distance\n   - Impact: May benefit from graph structure or better spatial encoding\n\n3. **Temporal Patterns**:\n   - Original: Diurnal and seasonal cycles\n   - Traffic: Multiple periodicities (daily, weekly, holidays)\n   - Impact: Current temporal encoding should work well\n\n**Expected Deltas**:\n- MAE: +10-20% higher than SOTA GNNs (acceptable for 30x fewer parameters)\n- Training time: 50-70% faster than GNN models\n- Convergence: Similar or faster due to simpler architecture\n"""\n\n    print(table)\n    return table\n\n\ndef generate_recommendations():\n    """Generate recommendations for improvement"""\n\n    print("\\n" + "="*80)\n    print("RECOMMENDATIONS & FOLLOW-UP EXPERIMENTS")\n    print("="*80)\n\n    recommendations = """\n## Recommendations for Optimal STELLA Performance\n\n### Immediate Actions\n1. **Run Actual Benchmark**: Execute training on METR-LA to get real metrics\n   ```bash\n   cd Bigscity-LibCity\n   python run_model.py --task traffic_state_pred --model STELLA --dataset METR_LA\n   ```\n\n2. **Hyperparameter Tuning**: Experiment with key parameters\n   - `d_model`: Try [32, 64, 128] - higher may capture traffic complexity better\n   - `num_layer`: Try [2, 3, 4] - more layers for complex patterns\n   - `learning_rate`: Try [0.0001, 0.0005, 0.001]\n   - `dropout`: Try [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]\n\n3. **Feature Engineering**:\n   - Ensure temporal features are included (time-of-day, day-of-week)\n   - Add external features if available (weather, events)\n   - Use node positions if available (set `if_rel=False`)\n\n### Follow-Up Experiments\n\n#### Experiment 1: Architecture Scaling\n**Objective**: Find optimal model size for traffic data\n\n| Config | d_model | num_layer | Parameters | Expected MAE |\n|--------|---------|-----------|------------|--------------|\n| Small | 16 | 1 | ~7K | ~4.2 |\n| Medium | 32 | 2 | ~14K | ~3.8 |\n| Large | 64 | 3 | ~58K | ~3.5 |\n| XLarge | 128 | 4 | ~230K | ~3.3 |\n\n**Action**: Run grid search with automation helper\n```python\nfrom automation.stella_integration_helper import create_experiment_config\nconfigs = create_experiment_config(param_grid={\n    \'d_model\': [16, 32, 64, 128],\n    \'num_layer\': [1, 2, 3, 4]\n})\n```\n\n#### Experiment 2: Spatial Encoding Comparison\n**Objective**: Compare position-based vs learnable embeddings\n\n| Config | if_rel | Use Case | Expected Impact |\n|--------|--------|----------|-----------------|\n| Config A | False | With node positions | Better if positions informative |\n| Config B | True | Learnable embeddings | Better if topology complex |\n\n#### Experiment 3: Multi-Dataset Validation\n**Objective**: Validate across different traffic patterns\n\n| Dataset | Nodes | Pattern | Expected MAE |\n|---------|-------|---------|--------------|\n| METR_LA | 207 | Urban | 3.8 |\n| PEMS_BAY | 325 | Highway | 1.9 |\n| PEMSD4 | 307 | Mixed | 2.5 |\n| PEMSD8 | 170 | Regional | 2.2 |\n\n#### Experiment 4: Training Strategy Optimization\n**Objective**: Optimize convergence and final performance\n\n| Strategy | Learning Rate | Scheduler | Warmup | Expected Improvement |\n|----------|--------------|-----------|--------|---------------------|\n| Baseline | 0.0005 | MultiStep | No | - |\n| Strategy A | 0.001 | Cosine | Yes | +5-10% faster |\n| Strategy B | 0.0005 | OneCycle | Yes | +3-5% better |\n| Strategy C | 0.0003 | ReduceOnPlateau | No | +2-3% better |\n\n### If Results Diverge Significantly\n\n**Scenario 1: MAE > 20% higher than estimated**\n\nPossible causes:\n1. Missing temporal features\n2. Suboptimal hyperparameters\n3. Data preprocessing issues\n4. Insufficient training epochs\n\nActions:\n1. Verify dataset loading (check batch shapes, feature presence)\n2. Increase model capacity (d_model=64, num_layer=3)\n3. Add learning rate warmup\n4. Train for more epochs (150-200)\n\n**Scenario 2: Training not converging**\n\nPossible causes:\n1. Learning rate too high\n2. Gradient explosion\n3. Batch normalization needed\n4. Data scaling issues\n\nActions:\n1. Reduce learning rate to 0.0001\n2. Enable gradient clipping (already in config)\n3. Check data normalization\n4. Add batch normalization to MLP layers\n\n**Scenario 3: Overfitting (train loss << val loss)**\n\nPossible causes:\n1. Model too complex for data\n2. Insufficient regularization\n3. Data leakage\n\nActions:\n1. Increase dropout (0.3 or 0.4)\n2. Add weight decay\n3. Use early stopping (already in config)\n4. Reduce model size\n\n### Corrective Steps Checklist\n\nIf performance is unsatisfactory:\n\n- [ ] Verify data loading and preprocessing\n- [ ] Check temporal feature inclusion\n- [ ] Validate input/output shapes\n- [ ] Try different hyperparameters\n- [ ] Increase model capacity\n- [ ] Extend training duration\n- [ ] Compare with baseline models on same data\n- [ ] Analyze failure cases (which time steps, which nodes)\n- [ ] Visualize predictions vs ground truth\n- [ ] Check for data quality issues\n\n### Success Metrics\n\n**Minimum Acceptable Performance** (vs SOTA):\n- MAE: Within 30% of best model\n- Training time: 50%+ faster\n- Parameters: 90%+ fewer\n\n**Good Performance** (vs SOTA):\n- MAE: Within 15% of best model\n- Training time: 60%+ faster\n- Parameters: 95%+ fewer\n\n**Excellent Performance** (vs SOTA):\n- MAE: Within 10% of best model\n- Training time: 70%+ faster\n- Parameters: 97%+ fewer\n\nSTELLA\'s value proposition is the speed/accuracy trade-off, not absolute accuracy.\n"""\n\n    print(recommendations)\n    return recommendations\n\n\ndef save_analysis_report():\n    """Save complete analysis report"""\n\n    metrics, characteristics = create_metrics_report()\n    comparison_table = generate_comparison_table()\n    recommendations = generate_recommendations()\n\n    report = f"""# STELLA Model - Comprehensive Metrics & Analysis Report\n\n**Date**: {time.strftime(\'%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\')}\n**Model**: STELLA (Spatial-Temporal Embedded Lightweight Model)\n**Paper**: NeurIPS 2025\n**Integration**: LibCity\n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThis report provides:\n1. **Estimated Performance Metrics** - Based on model characteristics and baselines\n2. **Comparison with SOTA Models** - How STELLA compares to GNN/Tra